# Noelle Documentation Index
<!-- noelle-index:v3; scope: global; generated: YYYY-MM-DD; validated: YYYY-MM-DD; schema: noelle-index/v3; projects: N; coverage: full|partial -->

> Global routing map for all projects served by Noelle. Read this file first,
> then open only the project indexes and source documents needed for the user's
> question.
>
> Retrieval budget: read at most 2 project indexes and 5 source documents before
> falling back to read-only search or reporting insufficient evidence. Stop as
> soon as the primary source and required supporting sources support the answer.

## Retrieval protocol

1. Normalize the question: identify project names, aliases, exact identifiers,
   and whether it spans projects.
2. Route with the `Query router` and `Cross-project topic map` below.
3. Read `docs/<PROJECT_FOLDER>/index.md` for each selected project.
4. Read the selected project's `AGENTS.md` immediately after its index.
5. Use the project `Topic map` to select the primary source and only the
   supporting sources needed for the answer.
6. Stop when the evidence supports the answer. Do not open the whole docs tree.
7. Fall back to read-only file search when routing is missing, stale, or
   uncertain. State the limitation when it affects the answer.
8. Cite exact paths and section headings.

## Query router

| Query type or intent | Primary route | Supporting route | Stop condition |
|---|---|---|---|
| What is this? | Project snapshot and topic map | Glossary, overview | Snapshot plus one topic source |
| How do I do this? | Project topic map -> workflow | Runbook, setup guide | Procedure plus verification |
| Why does it work this way? | Architecture or decision topic | ADR, design document | Decision plus evidence |
| It is not working | Troubleshooting topic | Runbook, errors, code | Cause, fix, and verification |
| API or configuration reference | Contract or configuration topic | Schema, reference | Contract plus invariant |
| Cross-project behavior | Cross-project topic map | Selected project topic maps | One primary source per project |
| Historical or deprecated behavior | History or stale topic | Changelog, archive, current source | Superseded source plus current source |

## Project routing

| Project | Documentation folder | Status | Description | Use for | Do not use for | Aliases and keywords | Project instructions | Best modes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| <Project name> | `docs/<PROJECT_FOLDER>/` | active | <One-sentence description> | <Question types and user intents> | <Nearby topics owned elsewhere> | <Names, products, features> | `<project-root>/AGENTS.md` or `none` | Normal, Artist, Dev |

Project instructions are high-priority context for that project only. They must
never be merged with another project's instructions.

## Cross-project topic map

Use this only for topics that span projects or share ambiguous terminology. Each
row must name one primary project and source. Keep this map small.

| Topic | Aliases or exact identifiers | Primary project and source | Supporting projects and sources | Stale or conflicting | Search terms | Stop condition |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| <Shared topic> | <Names, codes, API fields, synonyms> | `<PROJECT_FOLDER>` -> `<path>` -> <section> | `<PROJECT_FOLDER>` -> `<path>` -> <section> | `<path>` -> <section> (stale) | `<term1>|<term2>` | <What to read before stopping> |

Do not copy detailed project content into this table. Route to the project topic
maps and source documents.

## Cross-project relationships

Use typed relationships instead of vague "related" links.

- `depends_on`: <Project A> requires <Project B> for <boundary>.
- `reads_from`: <Project A> consumes data or artifacts from <Project B>.
- `writes_to`: <Project A> produces data or artifacts used by <Project B>.
- `shares_concept`: Both projects use <shared concept>, but define it in their own scope.
- `supersedes`: <New project or source> replaces <old project or source>.
- `conflicts_with`: Sources disagree; read both and report the conflict.

## Global glossary and disambiguation

- **<Term>**: <Meaning>. Route to `<PROJECT_FOLDER>` and `<path>` -> <section>.
- **<Abbreviation>**: <Expanded name>. Also called <aliases>.
- **<Ambiguous name>**: Use `<Project A>` when <condition>; use `<Project B>` when <condition>.

## Authority, freshness, and conflict rules

Within a project, use this default order unless the project index declares a
more specific rule:

1. Noelle's system and security rules.
2. The project's applicable `AGENTS.md`, limited to that project.
3. Documents marked `canonical` and currently verified.
4. Documents marked `supporting` or `reference`.
5. Navigational indexes and generated summaries.
6. Planning, historical, stale, superseded, optional, or proposed material.

An index is a navigation aid, not proof of a detailed fact. If sources conflict,
identify the conflict, prefer the more specific and recently verified source,
and cite the evidence. Never silently use a stale or superseded source as
current truth. Skip optional material unless the answer requires it.

## Out of scope and abstention

- <Topic not covered by any project>
- <Question that requires access or permissions Noelle does not have>
- <Action Noelle is not allowed to perform>

When no route provides sufficient evidence, say what was not found and cite the
closest available project or document rather than inventing an answer.

